`editableIds` asks the `UserRecordAccess` object which records the running user can edit, so ownership, sharing, the role hierarchy and View All/Modify All are all handled by the platform. The IDs are queried in chunks of 200, so 2,000 IDs use exactly 10 SOQL queries.

File: force-app/main/default/classes/RecordAccessService.cls
```apex
public with sharing class RecordAccessService {

    // UserRecordAccess accepts at most 200 RecordIds per query
    private static final Integer CHUNK_SIZE = 200;

    public static Set<Id> editableIds(Set<Id> recordIds) {
        Set<Id> result = new Set<Id>();
        if (recordIds == null || recordIds.isEmpty()) {
            return result;
        }

        Id userId = UserInfo.getUserId();
        List<Id> pending = new List<Id>();
        for (Id recId : recordIds) {
            if (recId != null) {
                pending.add(recId);
            }
        }

        Integer total = pending.size();
        for (Integer start = 0; start < total; start += CHUNK_SIZE) {
            Set<Id> chunk = new Set<Id>();
            Integer stop = Math.min(start + CHUNK_SIZE, total);
            for (Integer i = start; i < stop; i++) {
                chunk.add(pending[i]);
            }
            for (UserRecordAccess ura : [
                SELECT RecordId, HasEditAccess
                FROM UserRecordAccess
                WHERE UserId = :userId AND RecordId IN :chunk
            ]) {
                if (ura.HasEditAccess) {
                    result.add(ura.RecordId);
                }
            }
        }
        return result;
    }
}
```

- **Null IDs:** any null entries in the input set are skipped before querying.
- **No extra query cost for mixed objects:** IDs from different objects can share a chunk, because one `UserRecordAccess` query can cover several object types.
- **Edit filter applied in Apex:** each query reads `HasEditAccess` and the editable IDs are picked out in code. This is because `UserRecordAccess` restricts filtering on its access fields in the `WHERE` clause.

Approach: check object read access, then build the query from only the fields the user can read, and run it in user mode.

- **Missing object access:** If the user cannot read `FB_Employee__c`, the method returns an empty list before querying.
- **Field selection:** `Id` and `Name` are always selected. Each of the three custom fields is added only if `DescribeFieldResult.isAccessible()` is true for the running user. Fields that are never queried are absent from the returned records, not present with a null value.
- **User mode:** The query runs with `AccessLevel.USER_MODE`. That enforces sharing, CRUD and FLS no matter how API-version defaults change. Every selected field has already passed the readability check, so the query does not throw.
- **Why not `stripInaccessible`:** I chose not to query everything and then call `Security.stripInaccessible`. That pattern needs a query that skips FLS, and how such a query treats sharing depends on the API version and class keywords. Building the field list first avoids that question.
- **Dynamic query safety:** The only values put into the SOQL string are hardcoded field names (no user input). That rules out SOQL injection.

File: force-app/main/default/classes/EmployeeDirectoryService.cls
```apex
public with sharing class EmployeeDirectoryService {

    private static final List<Schema.SObjectField> OPTIONAL_FIELDS = new List<Schema.SObjectField>{
        FB_Employee__c.Department__c,
        FB_Employee__c.Salary__c,
        FB_Employee__c.Personal_Email__c
    };

    public static List<FB_Employee__c> getDirectory() {
        if (!Schema.SObjectType.FB_Employee__c.isAccessible()) {
            return new List<FB_Employee__c>();
        }

        List<String> fields = new List<String>{ 'Id', 'Name' };
        for (Schema.SObjectField f : OPTIONAL_FIELDS) {
            Schema.DescribeFieldResult dfr = f.getDescribe();
            if (dfr.isAccessible()) {
                fields.add(dfr.getName());
            }
        }

        // Field names come only from the hardcoded token list above (no user input),
        // so the dynamic query is not open to injection. USER_MODE enforces sharing,
        // object permissions and FLS; every selected field has been verified readable.
        String soql = 'SELECT ' + String.join(fields, ', ')
                    + ' FROM FB_Employee__c ORDER BY Name ASC';

        return (List<FB_Employee__c>) Database.queryWithBinds(
            soql,
            new Map<String, Object>(),
            AccessLevel.USER_MODE
        );
    }
}
