/**
 * Imports legacy contacts without creating welcome tasks.
 * Only TA_Contact_CreateWelcomeTask is bypassed; all other Contact automation
 * (e.g. TA_Contact_FormatPhone) still runs.
 */
public inherited sharing class ContactImportService {

    public static void importContacts(List<Contact> contacts) {
        if (contacts == null || contacts.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        System.Type welcomeAction = TA_Contact_CreateWelcomeTask.class;
        // Respect a bypass that a caller may already have set; only clear what we set.
        Boolean alreadyBypassed = MetadataTriggerHandler.isBypassed(welcomeAction);

        if (!alreadyBypassed) {
            MetadataTriggerHandler.bypass(welcomeAction);
        }
        try {
            Database.insert(contacts, true, AccessLevel.USER_MODE);
        } finally {
            if (!alreadyBypassed) {
                MetadataTriggerHandler.clearBypass(welcomeAction);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

The service updates the Accounts while the Account bypass is active, removes the bypass in `finally`, and only then updates the Contacts.

File: force-app/main/default/classes/AccountOwnershipService.cls
```apex
/**
 * Moves Accounts and all of their Contacts to a new owner.
 * Account trigger actions are skipped through the TAF transaction bypass.
 * Contact trigger actions run normally.
 */
public with sharing class AccountOwnershipService {

    public static void reassign(Set<Id> accountIds, Id newOwnerId) {
        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }
        // Drop null entries without changing the caller's set.
        Set<Id> ids = new Set<Id>(accountIds);
        ids.remove(null);
        if (ids.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // SOQL query 1 of 1.
        List<Contact> contacts = [
            SELECT Id
            FROM Contact
            WHERE AccountId IN :ids
        ];

        List<Account> accounts = new List<Account>();
        for (Id accountId : ids) {
            accounts.add(new Account(Id = accountId, OwnerId = newOwnerId));
        }
        for (Contact c : contacts) {
            c.OwnerId = newOwnerId;
        }

        // Bypass Account trigger actions for the Account DML only.
        // Keep any bypass the caller had already set.
        Boolean wasBypassed = TriggerBase.isBypassed(Account.SObjectType);
        if (!wasBypassed) {
            TriggerBase.bypass(Account.SObjectType);
        }
        try {
            update accounts; // DML 1: all-or-none
        } finally {
            if (!wasBypassed) {
                TriggerBase.clearBypass(Account.SObjectType);
            }
        }

        // The Account bypass is cleared here, so Contact actions and any
        // Account updates they cause behave normally.
        if (!contacts.isEmpty()) {
            update contacts; // DML 2: all-or-none
        }
    }
}
```

- **Bypass and cleanup:** the Account bypass covers only the Account update. It is removed in `finally`, so Account automation behaves normally afterwards whether the update succeeds or throws. Exceptions are not caught, so they reach the caller unchanged.
- **Caller's existing bypass:** if the caller had already bypassed Account, the service leaves that bypass in place rather than removing it.
- **Contact update:** this runs after the bypass is cleared, so all Contact actions run normally. If those actions update Accounts (for example rollups), the Account actions run normally too.
- **Limits:**
  - **Queries and DML:** one SOQL query (Contacts) and at most two DML statements, whatever the volume. The Accounts are built in memory from their Ids, so no Account query is needed.
  - **Row limit:** very large books (more than about 10,000 Contacts in total) would still hit the DML row limit.
- **Sharing:** the class is declared `with sharing`, so the running user must have access to the records being reassigned.

